---
description: Keep Cursor/agent shell work from saturating CPU and RAM on this machine
alwaysApply: true
---

# Cursor resource limits (leave headroom)

This machine runs other apps (browser, VM, etc.) alongside Cursor. When a cgroup
or systemd slice caps Cursor/agent CPU and memory, agents must still avoid
pointless full-machine load inside that cap.

## Parallelism

Follow `build-parallelism.mdc` for `BUILD_JOBS` — do not duplicate that formula here.

- Prefer **one** ripgrep/glob at a time; narrow paths instead of searching `$HOME` or `.`
- Do **not** run broad `rg`/`find` over the home directory, worktrees root, or multiple repos in parallel
- Cap explicit ripgrep threads: `rg --threads "$BUILD_JOBS"` (or fewer)
- Do not spawn multiple background agents/subagents unless the user asked for parallelism

## Search scope

- Search inside the **smallest relevant directory** (a package, module, or repo), not the workspace root when root is `~`
- Respect `.gitignore`, `.cursorignore`, and existing `search.exclude` / `files.watcherExclude`
- Never use `rg --follow` / symlink-following scans across large trees
- Prefer `Grep` with `glob` / `head_limit` over unbounded shell ripgrep

## Memory

- Avoid loading huge files, full directory listings, or giant command outputs into context
- Do not start heavy builds, test suites, or indexers in parallel with other agent work unless requested

## When the machine is already struggling

If swap is high or the UI is stuttering: finish the current step, avoid new searches/builds, and tell the user before launching anything expensive.

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — scans the whole home tree from workspace root
rg --follow 'pattern' .

# ❌ BAD — two repo-wide searches in parallel
rg -l foo ~/git/repo-a & rg -l bar ~/git/repo-b & wait

# ✅ GOOD — one search, smallest useful directory
rg --threads "$BUILD_JOBS" 'pattern' ~/git/dreambox/src

# ✅ GOOD — use the editor search tool with a glob and head limit
# Grep(pattern="foo", glob="**/*.py", head_limit=50)
```
